        {
          "id": "37888f2f-4536-36d4-ac3f-0aee81406290",
          "content": {
            "id": "37888f2f-4536-36d4-ac3f-0aee81406290",
            "contentType": "STORY",
            "title": "One-third of firms paused or delayed stainless steel orders due to tariffs, Outokumpu says",
            "description": "",
            "summary": "(Reuters) -At least a third of surveyed companies have paused or delayed stainless steel orders because of U.S. import tariffs of up to 50%, while more than half are reassessing their sourcing strategy, Finnish steelmaker Outokumpu said on Monday.  Outokumpu President and CEO Kati ter Horst told Reuters the slowing global demand means current European Union import quotas are too high, and that she expects the European Commission to announce in October measures to curb imports of steel from rivals to protect domestic producers.  A third of businesses switched steel suppliers as of May, while the addition in August of hundreds of derivative products to the U.S. list of goods subject to the levies created uncertainty for equipment and machinery buyers, ter Horst said.",
